WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.599 --> 00:00:05.529
After 95 uncanny years spent imagining his
way to the top of the world, Stan Lee passed

00:00:05.529 --> 00:00:07.860
away on November 12, 2018.

00:00:08.390 --> 00:00:12.260
It's only now that what appears to be the
final cameo from the Marvel Comics figurehead

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:16.120
is seeing release, and the story of that cameo
appearance may surprise you.

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:18.260
For one thing, it's not in a Marvel movie.

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:23.740
If you know Stan Lee, you've probably also
heard of Jason Mewes - as far as fanbases

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:25.900
are concerned, there's a lot of overlap there.

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:30.900
Mewes came up as an actor in the '90s as the
taller, lankier, louder half of Jay and Silent

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:35.699
Bob, the dynamic duo of drug dealers who helped
tie together the early films of writer-director

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:37.980
Kevin Smith - a.k.a. Silent Bob.

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:44.070
After preparing for the project since 2016,
Jason Mewes has finally launched his own directorial

00:00:44.070 --> 00:00:48.719
debut feature, Madness in the Method, to be
released in theaters and on-demand on August

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:50.140
2, 2019.

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:54.620
After charting an unexpected path through
show business toward status as a bona fide

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:58.899
icon, Mewes was able to call on many other
pop culture players for help when it came

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:00.340
time to direct his own movie.

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:05.040
That roster includes Stan Lee, who agreed
to shoot what Mewes calls his favorite scene

00:01:05.049 --> 00:01:09.000
in the movie - not just for the content of
the sequence, but for the fact that Lee came

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:10.260
out to do the scene at all.

00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:14.560
We sat down with Mewes for an exclusive interview
and he described the experience of filming

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:16.750
Lee as being, quote, "just surreal."

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:21.200
The opportunity came together in a rapid fashion
that required the movie's crew to rush in

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:25.700
three and a half hours early in order to set
up Lee's shots - and for a heartwarming reason,

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:26.360
too.

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:31.420
“He had to be out of there by 5:30, because
he will not miss dinner with his wife.

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:35.810
Every night at 6:00 he has dinner with his
wife, and he will not miss that."

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:40.760
"Her name was Joan, and I took Joanie to lunch,
and then we got married.

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:41.840
It was a good lunch."

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:47.030
To see what Stan Lee's scene in Madness in
the Method actually entails, you'll have to

00:01:47.030 --> 00:01:48.380
watch the movie for yourself.

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:52.810
But it shouldn't be too surprising that the
sequence at least partially involves Lee delivering

00:01:52.810 --> 00:01:54.880
a personal insult to Jason Mewes.

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:56.220
"Jason Mewes?

00:01:56.500 --> 00:01:58.160
That smoochie-boochie guy?"

00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:01.000
But that animosity is just a product of the
screenplay.

00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:05.439
According to Mewes, the real-life Lee was
a saint on set, asking little in return for

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:06.689
his appearance in the film.

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:14.400
"To me it seemed such a sweet gesture for
him to roll out of his house to come down

00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:18.420
and be in our movie when he's not getting
paid a bunch of money.

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:21.020
And just be like, 'Hey, we're going to do
this favor for Mewes.'

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:23.620
So to me it was literally a favor.

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:26.200
Just, 'Hey, we want to be in Jay's movie.

00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:30.120
He's a nice kid, we've known him since Mallrats,
since '90-whatever.'

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:38.820
It just will always be a special, awesome
moment for me."

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:43.440
One aspect that makes the experience so special
is the distinct likelihood of it being Lee's

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:47.970
last cameo, unless some hidden material in
the Disney vaults sees release someday in

00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:48.940
Avengers 9.

00:02:49.270 --> 00:02:55.920
"I don't know for sure, but what it seems
like, my movie is the last movie he shot.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:59.160
it's just really amazing that he did
that."

00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:03.700
Mewes met Stan Lee for the first time back
when "Stan Lee cameos" weren't really a thing.

00:03:03.980 --> 00:03:08.530
It was 1995, on the set of Mallrats, Kevin
Smith's second movie, and the first to be

00:03:08.530 --> 00:03:10.140
produced by a major studio.

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:16.920
“Clerks was the first movie, but to me,
Mallrats is my first movie, because it was

00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:18.480
like it became real.

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:23.790
Like I'm in Minnesota, staying in a hotel,
and there's wardrobe, and there's stunt guys,

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:29.080
and I get to hang from a grappling hook, and
I get to meet Stan Lee for the first time."

00:03:29.190 --> 00:03:32.440
It would probably have come as a surprise
to the younger Mewes to learn that he would

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:36.670
go on to meet Lee over and over again in the
years to come, as the two toured the same

00:03:36.670 --> 00:03:39.340
circles at comic book conventions and movie
sets.

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:43.440
But that doesn't mean he's not still proud
of his poster for Mallrats, signed by Lee

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:43.940
himself.

00:03:44.380 --> 00:03:50.200
"When I first met Stan, I think I got something
signed, way back in '95 or so when we made

00:03:50.210 --> 00:03:52.710
Mallrats, because I was so excited."

00:03:52.710 --> 00:03:57.640
That first piece of memorabilia got unfortunately
misplaced, but no matter - they'd meet again.

00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:02.660
"I saw him many, many times after that at
different comic book conventions.

00:04:02.660 --> 00:04:04.300
We did an Audi commercial.

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:05.960
Kevin directed, Stan was there."

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:10.020
Their relationship came full circle when Lee
made an appearance reading the script for

00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:15.610
Mallrats in 2019's Captain Marvel, effectively
immortalizing the movie, and Mewes himself,

00:04:15.610 --> 00:04:17.019
in the Marvel Cinematic Universe.

00:04:18.139 --> 00:04:21.709
Though Mewes may have had the privilege of
directing Stan Lee in his own movie, that's

00:04:21.709 --> 00:04:24.500
not necessarily his favorite of Lee's cameos.

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:26.620
"My favorite.

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:27.600
I don’t…

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:30.300
He does so many awesome ones."

00:04:30.440 --> 00:04:34.960
Some fans may love the one in The Amazing
Spider-Man in which Lee appears as a librarian,

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:39.020
blissfully arranging books with headphones
on while Spider-Man and the Lizard tear up

00:04:39.020 --> 00:04:39.920
the room behind him.

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:44.560
Others may enjoy the moment in The Incredible
Hulk in which Lee accidentally drinks a soda

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:46.900
tainted with Bruce Banner's radioactive blood.

00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:51.240
But the one that comes to Mewes' mind as a
favorite cameo is from another movie helmed

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:53.420
by longtime collaborator Kevin Smith.

00:04:53.540 --> 00:04:59.160
"I really liked his cameo in Kevin's movie,
Yoga Hosers.

00:04:59.160 --> 00:05:01.100
He picks up the phone, and he's like…

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:03.420
I forget what he says, exactly.

00:05:03.420 --> 00:05:04.640
But it was really sweet."

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:09.100
Specifically, Lee is seen picking up the phone
as a police dispatcher in the teen horror

00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:13.620
movie, listening to the protagonists' stoned-sounding
ramblings about little sausage men that look

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:17.660
like Adolf Hitler – in Lee's book, one of
history's worst villains.

00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:23.400
"Well he sure is a nasty camper – worse
than Dr. Doom and Darth Vader combined."

00:05:24.020 --> 00:05:27.040
He then provides the movie's titular line
as an insult.

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:29.880
“G--damn Yoga Hosers."

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:34.360
When it comes to Stan Lee cameos, it's harder
to tell what the future holds than you might

00:05:34.360 --> 00:05:34.860
think.

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:39.160
Footage may exist that we don't know about,
planned for use in future films in ways we

00:05:39.170 --> 00:05:40.170
can't predict.

00:05:40.170 --> 00:05:44.500
But for now, it sure seems like Madness in
the Method is set to be Lee's last role.

00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:46.540
Not that people haven't been wrong before.

00:05:46.740 --> 00:05:51.280
Directors Anthony and Joe Russo have implied
in the past that Lee's cameo for Endgame was

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:55.669
the last one he shot - but that's an assertion
contradicted by the fact that Lee's cameo

00:05:55.669 --> 00:05:58.480
for Captain Marvel was actually filmed after
that.

00:05:58.780 --> 00:06:03.380
Of course, Captain Marvel was released before
Endgame, and both films came out months before

00:06:03.380 --> 00:06:07.180
Madness in the Method, even though the Madness
cameo was probably shot first.

00:06:07.300 --> 00:06:08.000
It's confusing.

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:12.200
When it comes to footage we don't know about
floating around, it's entirely possible that

00:06:12.200 --> 00:06:16.600
Madness in the Method may just feature the
last Stan Lee cameo to come out this year.

00:06:16.860 --> 00:06:21.720
But for now, as far as anyone can tell, Jason
Mewes' directorial debut holds the last bit

00:06:21.730 --> 00:06:26.580
of cameo footage to be released of a media
magnate who became a surprising, applause-worthy

00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:28.180
presence on the silver screen.

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:30.000
"Have you seen this cameo yet?”

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:30.500
“Yes.”

00:06:30.500 --> 00:06:31.000
“You have?

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:31.920
I haven't seen it yet!

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:35.000
Is it any – the only reason I came was to
see my cameo!"

